1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
This animal sees colors that do not have names.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,720
Not because no one has looked,

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:07,520
because human eyes are not built to receive them.

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:11,040
Human eyes carry three kinds of color receptor.

5
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:14,960
Every color you have ever seen was built from those three.

6
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:18,040
The mantis shrimp carries up to 16.

7
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:21,280
Remarkably, its eyes do not stop at color.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:25,320
Each one is mounted on its own independently moving stock,

9
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,160
aimed in a different direction,

10
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:30,680
processing a different part of the world at the same time.

11
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:33,600
What's extraordinary is that this system should not work

12
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:35,240
as well as it does.

13
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:37,480
Science has only recently discovered

14
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:39,920
why an animal would need to see this much

15
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,680
of the color spectrum in the first place.

16
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,480
We'll come back to that.

17
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:48,600
But first, there is a stranger version of this eye.

18
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:53,560
Most animals, including humans, see light only as color.

19
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:58,200
The mantis shrimp also sees the angle light waves twist through,

20
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,840
a property called polarization,

21
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:04,160
invisible to nearly every other eye on Earth.

22
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:05,920
Of all the creatures on Earth,

23
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:09,600
only a small handful can detect this directly.

24
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:12,240
The mantis shrimp is the only one known

25
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,040
to detect circular polarization,

26
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:18,480
a twisting pattern of light that does not occur naturally

27
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,320
anywhere else in the animal kingdom.

28
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:23,480
Researchers believe some mantis shrimp

29
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:26,200
use this hidden channel to signal to each other

30
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,760
in a code no predator can read,

31
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:31,320
because no predator's eyes can see it.

32
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,120
But the eyes are not even the most anomalous part

33
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:35,440
of this animal.

34
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,040
What they are built to track is the mantis shrimp strike

35
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:42,320
is one of the fastest movements in the animal kingdom,

36
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:45,920
accelerating so quickly that boils the water in front of it

37
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,640
for a fraction of a second,

38
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:52,360
producing a flash of light from the collapsing bubble alone.

39
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:53,960
And its eyes.

40
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:58,640
The same 16 receptor polarization reading eyes

41
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,200
are fast enough to track the entire event in real time,

42
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,480
while every other eye nearby only sees a blur.

43
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:08,800
An eye built to see a spectrum with no name,

44
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,960
watching a strike too fast for anything else to follow.

45
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:15,240
What we now know is that this animal

46
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,680
was never just hiding in a burrow.

47
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:22,360
It was watching everything in colors none of us will ever see.

